WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.133
Hello.

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:01.468
I'm Dana Bash in Washington,

00:00:01.468 --> 00:00:02.635
where the state of our union

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:03.970
is thankful,

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.372
thankful that an American

00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:07.073
detained in a Russian penal

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:08.975
colony is finally home.

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:09.509
You're looking

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:11.311
at New pictures of Brittney Griner,

00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:12.946
the WNBA superstar

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:15.215
who touched down on American soil early

00:00:15.215 --> 00:00:16.383
Friday morning.

00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:17.784
She reunited with her wife,

00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:19.819
Cheryl, and was taken immediately

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:21.821
for a medical evaluation.

00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:23.990
President Biden celebrated her return

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:25.325
and now faces questions

00:00:25.325 --> 00:00:27.227
about his decision to trade.

00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:28.094
Convicted and

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:30.196
convicted Russian arms dealer

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:33.099
Viktor Bout in exchange for the WNBA star

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:35.769
and the American left behind in Russia.

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:36.536
Paul Wieland,

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:38.304
who has been held prisoner there

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:39.806
for nearly four years,

00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:41.741
My guess now is working to free

00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:44.144
wheeling and dozens of Americans

00:00:44.144 --> 00:00:46.413
held hostage and other foreign nations.

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:48.615
And he has a pretty good track record.

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:49.649
Special envoy

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:50.884
special presidential envoy,

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:52.786
I should say, for hostage affairs

00:00:52.786 --> 00:00:54.120
Roger Carstens

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.955
helped secure Griner's release

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.390
and was there to greet her

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.425
and bring her home.

00:00:58.425 --> 00:00:59.492
Thank you so much.

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:01.261
For joining me this morning.

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:03.963
What an ordeal and what a saga.

00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:05.331
Let's start

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:07.133
with the sort of the personal.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:09.769
You flew to the dump to UAE.

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:11.671
You welcomed her on the tarmac.

00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:14.707
What was the moment like

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:15.475
for both of you

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:16.643
and what was it like for her

00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:18.311
or specifically?

00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:20.647
It's always kind of an exciting moment

00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:22.382
when you jump on the other country's

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:24.684
plane and walk up to a person,

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:25.685
in this case, Brittney.

00:01:25.685 --> 00:01:26.519
And I'll tell you what I told her.

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:27.353
I said, Brittney,

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:29.355
my name is Ambassador Roger. Carstens.

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:29.956
I'm with the U.S.

00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:31.391
Department of State

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:32.525
on behalf of the president

00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:34.160
of the United States, Joe Biden,

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.029
and Secretary of State Tony Blinken.

00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:37.764
I'm here to take you home.

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:38.598
And at that moment,

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:39.265
I think

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:40.467
every person finally starts

00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:41.968
to realize that it's going to happen.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:42.602
It's setting in.

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.471
Certainly, Brittney felt that way.

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:45.305
And at that point,

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:46.573
we have to go through a little more

00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:47.540
of the choreography

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:48.441
to get her on the plane

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:49.976
usually takes about 3 minutes.

00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:50.877
But here's what I wouldn't mind

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:51.644
telling you.

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:54.114
When she finally got onto the US plane,

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:54.948
I said, Brittney,

00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:56.049
you must have been through a lot

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:57.717
over the last ten months.

00:01:57.717 --> 00:01:58.751
Here's your seat.

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:00.253
Please feel free to decompress.

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:01.788
Will give you your space.

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:02.822
And she said, oh, no,

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:04.357
I've been in prison for ten months now

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:06.326
listening to Russian. I want to talk.

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:08.228
But first of all, who are these guys?

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:09.462
And she moved right past me

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:11.631
and went to every member on that crew,

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:12.632
looked him in the eyes,

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:13.533
shook their hands

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:14.367
and asked about them,

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:15.502
got their names,

00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:17.437
making a personal connection with them.

00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:18.505
It was really amazing.

00:02:18.505 --> 00:02:20.974
And then later on, on an 18 hour flight,

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:22.542
she probably spent 12 hours

00:02:22.542 --> 00:02:24.010
just just talking.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.045
We talked about everything under the sun,

00:02:26.045 --> 00:02:26.913
and I was left

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:27.780
with the impression

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.282
that this is an intelligent,

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:31.951
passionate, compassionate, humble,

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:34.954
interesting person, a patriotic person,

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:37.223
but above all, authentic

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:39.159
I hate the fact that I had to meet her

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:40.093
in this manner,

00:02:40.093 --> 00:02:41.161
but I actually felt blessed

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:42.996
having had a chance to get to know her.

00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:44.797
You said that she wanted to talk.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:46.099
Did you talk specifically

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:47.167
about the experience

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:48.635
in the Russian penal colony?

00:02:48.635 --> 00:02:49.469
She did.

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:50.470
I'd hate to steal her thunder

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.438
because it's her story to tell,

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:53.206
but she

00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:54.374
she spoke at length about what

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:55.909
it was like to go undergo that

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:56.643
ten month ordeal.

00:02:57.877 --> 00:02:59.045
Just going

00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:00.246
back to the first thing you said

00:03:00.246 --> 00:03:01.247
when you shook her hand

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:02.549
and you said that you were

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:04.150
there to take her home.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:04.884
Did she know?

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:06.319
Obviously, she was on a plane.

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:08.021
So she knew something was happening.

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:10.323
Did she know for sure she was being freed

00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:11.357
before that moment?

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:12.725
At that point, you did.

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:13.760
The Russians,

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:14.727
just like the United States,

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:15.528
has to go through certain

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:16.930
administrative procedures

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:17.564
at a certain point,

00:03:17.564 --> 00:03:19.499
it kind of becomes evident that something

00:03:19.499 --> 00:03:20.700
something's happening.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:21.634
And then usually

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:22.602
when the Russians pick up

00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:23.436
someone from their prison

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:24.637
cell, in this case, Brittany,

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:25.338
they give her a sense

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:26.406
that she's going home that day.

00:03:26.406 --> 00:03:26.839
What's that

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:28.374
like to be the person

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:30.009
to shake someone's hand

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:32.812
and welcome them back to America

00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:34.447
after they've been wrongly held?

00:03:34.447 --> 00:03:35.949
It's humbling.

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:36.716
I'm very grateful

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:37.684
that President Biden

00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:39.752
allows me a chance to do this job.

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:41.454
It's also a painful job.

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:42.255
So when you get the chance

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:42.589
to shake

00:03:42.589 --> 00:03:43.623
someone's hands, it's

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:45.024
one of those rare moments

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:46.693
that you get to celebrate a victory

00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:47.360
but know this.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.095
Even as we're welcoming someone home,

00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:50.330
we still have work to do.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.098
So as I'm shaking Brittany's hands

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:53.399
and we're taking to the aircraft

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:55.068
and having this great conversation,

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:56.269
my brain is already thinking about

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:56.903
Paul Whalen.

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:57.937
What can we do to get him back?

00:03:57.937 --> 00:03:58.738
What's our next move?

00:03:58.738 --> 00:03:59.739
What's the strategy?

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:00.373
How can we adapt?

00:04:00.373 --> 00:04:01.474
And I want to ask you that one second

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:03.543
just quickly. How's her physical health?

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:05.945
She's she looks great.

00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:07.647
I mean, she was full of energy,

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:08.648
looked fantastic.

00:04:08.648 --> 00:04:09.916
She's in Fort Sam Houston

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:11.217
right now, undergoing some

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:12.652
some medical evaluations.

00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:13.853
But she seems to be just fine.

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:16.022
Okay, so let's talk about Paul Whalen.

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:18.658
What options realistically

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.827
do you have to bring him home?

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:22.028
I would love to tell you about it

00:04:22.028 --> 00:04:22.562
because to me,

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:23.830
they're very exciting and interesting.

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:25.698
But we have to in these negotiations

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:26.933
because they're ongoing,

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:28.167
we usually have to keep our cards

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:30.737
close to. But are there cards?

00:04:30.737 --> 00:04:31.838
There's always cards.

00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:34.274
The options are always being evaluated.

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:36.209
We have to adapt at times

00:04:36.209 --> 00:04:36.809
But here's the thing

00:04:36.809 --> 00:04:37.877
I would like to leave you with.

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:39.545
We have an ongoing open dialog

00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:40.613
with the Russians,

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:41.447
and we have the commitment

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:41.714
of this

00:04:41.714 --> 00:04:43.616
president and my office

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:45.852
certainly to bring Paul Whalen home.

00:04:45.852 --> 00:04:46.486
To my mind.

00:04:46.486 --> 00:04:47.887
And I talked to Paul, by the way.

00:04:47.887 --> 00:04:49.055
I may not have mentioned this,

00:04:49.055 --> 00:04:50.523
but I talked to him on Friday,

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:51.958
the day after the swap.

00:04:51.958 --> 00:04:53.092
And here's what I told him.

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:54.093
I said, Paul,

00:04:54.093 --> 00:04:55.695
you have the commitment of this president

00:04:55.695 --> 00:04:56.729
the president's focus,

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:57.930
the secretary of state's focused.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.399
I'm certainly focused,

00:04:59.399 --> 00:05:00.933
and we're going to bring you home.

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:01.734
And I reminded him,

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:03.002
I said, Paul, when you're in the Marines

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:04.137
and I was in the Army,

00:05:04.137 --> 00:05:05.972
they always reminded you, keep the faith.

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:07.173
And I said, keep the faith.

00:05:07.173 --> 00:05:08.074
We're coming to get you,

00:05:08.074 --> 00:05:09.509
because I want

00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:11.144
our viewers to listen to something.

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:12.211
He actually called

00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:13.146
our colleague,

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:15.782
Jennifer Handler from the Russian prison.

00:05:16.049 --> 00:05:17.350
And here's what he said to her.

00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:20.953
I'm greatly disappointed

00:05:21.421 --> 00:05:23.189
that more has not been done

00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:24.724
to secure my release.

00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:26.292
I'm happy that

00:05:26.292 --> 00:05:28.194
Britney is going home today

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:30.730
and that Trevor went home when he did.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:31.864
But I don't understand why

00:05:31.864 --> 00:05:33.900
I'm still sitting here.

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:34.967
What's it like to hear that

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:36.636
I'm guessing he said similar to you?

00:05:36.636 --> 00:05:37.036
He did.

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:38.171
He shared his frustration

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:39.806
and I explained him to him

00:05:39.806 --> 00:05:40.640
that Paul,

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:41.908
this was a case where it was either

00:05:41.908 --> 00:05:43.209
one or not.

00:05:43.209 --> 00:05:44.744
We weren't able to get you out on this.

00:05:44.744 --> 00:05:45.211
Go round.

00:05:45.211 --> 00:05:46.446
We could not get the deal

00:05:46.446 --> 00:05:47.814
with the Russians.

00:05:47.814 --> 00:05:48.881
But had we not made the deal,

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:50.049
then you would not have come home.

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:51.284
There was no opportunity

00:05:51.284 --> 00:05:52.652
to bring you home at this time.

00:05:52.652 --> 00:05:54.520
But, Paul, we're coming for you.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:56.823
And what do you say to people like

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:58.391
Senator Bob Menendez,

00:05:58.391 --> 00:05:58.591
who's

00:05:58.591 --> 00:05:59.792
the chairman of the Foreign

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:01.060
Relations Committee,

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.662
one of the president's fellow Democrats,

00:06:02.662 --> 00:06:03.696
who said that

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:05.131
making that swap with Viktor

00:06:05.131 --> 00:06:06.632
Bout was deeply disturbing.

00:06:06.632 --> 00:06:08.201
He said, we must stop

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:10.069
inviting dictatorial and rogue

00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:11.504
regimes to use Americans

00:06:11.504 --> 00:06:13.406
overseas as bargaining chips.

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:15.575
Do you

00:06:16.342 --> 00:06:16.943
I guess, how do you

00:06:16.943 --> 00:06:18.544
respond to his argument

00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:20.179
that this sends a message

00:06:20.179 --> 00:06:21.481
to hostile countries

00:06:21.481 --> 00:06:22.415
that all they need to do

00:06:22.415 --> 00:06:23.416
is kidnap an American

00:06:23.416 --> 00:06:24.617
to get what they want

00:06:24.617 --> 00:06:26.419
Well, I'd say that it's important

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:27.854
to note that

00:06:28.321 --> 00:06:29.188
people that are held

00:06:29.188 --> 00:06:30.957
overseas are important to us.

00:06:30.957 --> 00:06:32.759
And that's kind of the word I'd say.

00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:33.159
I start

00:06:33.159 --> 00:06:34.794
when I look at the question morally,

00:06:34.794 --> 00:06:35.395
was it bad

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:36.796
to treat someone like Victor Boo?

00:06:36.796 --> 00:06:38.931
I think the question is it's horrific

00:06:38.931 --> 00:06:40.700
to leave an American wrongfully detained

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:41.667
in a foreign jail cell.

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:43.936
Is it going to incentivize

00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:45.438
other bad actors?

00:06:45.438 --> 00:06:46.105
Let me say this,

00:06:46.105 --> 00:06:47.673
but there are bad actors.

00:06:47.673 --> 00:06:48.674
We used to say the

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:49.942
the other side gets a vote.

00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:50.910
And in this case, it's

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:52.111
hard to keep these dictators

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:53.279
and these dictatorial governments

00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:54.547
as a chairman amendment,

00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:55.615
Menendez said,

00:06:55.615 --> 00:06:56.616
from taking Americans

00:06:56.616 --> 00:06:57.250
and trying to use them

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:58.518
as bargaining chips.

00:06:58.518 --> 00:07:00.052
But this is precisely why

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:00.620
this summer

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:02.522
the president signed an executive order

00:07:02.522 --> 00:07:04.223
that provides us with new tools

00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:06.159
to add a deterrence effect.

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:07.160
And additionally,

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:07.927
Secretary Blinken

00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:09.362
has been working with other countries

00:07:09.362 --> 00:07:11.497
to try to create a multilateral coalition

00:07:11.864 --> 00:07:12.999
that can put this

00:07:12.999 --> 00:07:14.367
I guess you could say this This

00:07:15.835 --> 00:07:16.803
using Americans is

00:07:16.803 --> 00:07:17.804
wrong wrongly

00:07:17.804 --> 00:07:19.138
as bargaining chips, rather,

00:07:19.138 --> 00:07:20.673
to put that on the dustbin of history.

00:07:20.673 --> 00:07:21.808
So let me ask you about that,

00:07:21.808 --> 00:07:23.075
because he did sign

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:23.943
the president did sign

00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:24.777
an executive order.

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:25.511
And what it does is it

00:07:25.511 --> 00:07:27.747
authorizes sanctions and visa bans

00:07:28.114 --> 00:07:30.149
against people responsible for wrongly

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:32.485
wrongfully detaining Americans.

00:07:32.485 --> 00:07:33.553
As far as we can tell,

00:07:33.553 --> 00:07:35.354
it hasn't been used. Why not?

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:36.489
It hasn't yet.

00:07:36.589 --> 00:07:37.089
I'll say this,

00:07:37.089 --> 00:07:38.558
but sometimes you might be working

00:07:38.558 --> 00:07:39.258
with the country

00:07:39.258 --> 00:07:40.526
and you might have hostages

00:07:40.526 --> 00:07:41.894
that you have to keep working out.

00:07:41.894 --> 00:07:43.362
It may not be the right timing

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:45.565
and place to use a sanctioning authority.

00:07:45.565 --> 00:07:46.933
But believe me, we're working

00:07:46.933 --> 00:07:48.401
on target packages right now.

00:07:48.401 --> 00:07:49.469
It's something that we discuss

00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:50.336
all the time.

00:07:50.336 --> 00:07:51.471
And it's not going to be too long

00:07:51.471 --> 00:07:52.705
before you see something rolled out.

00:07:52.705 --> 00:07:53.606
Well, for example,

00:07:53.606 --> 00:07:55.408
the there's an African leaders

00:07:55.408 --> 00:07:57.844
summit here in Washington coming up.

00:07:57.844 --> 00:07:58.244
The State

00:07:58.244 --> 00:07:59.912
Department says that

00:07:59.912 --> 00:08:02.782
the president of Rwanda is wrongly,

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:04.016
wrongfully detaining

00:08:04.016 --> 00:08:05.551
an American will be used.

00:08:06.519 --> 00:08:07.220
These are these are

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:08.287
decisions that we're constantly

00:08:08.287 --> 00:08:09.689
reevaluating and talking about.

00:08:09.689 --> 00:08:10.990
I'd hate to get into future

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:11.757
talks about that

00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:13.092
because these are things

00:08:13.092 --> 00:08:15.094
that are constantly under negotiation.

00:08:15.361 --> 00:08:16.863
And I'll just leave it at that, if I may.

00:08:16.863 --> 00:08:20.099
I want to ask about the other families

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:21.567
and the other hostages who are out.

00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:22.635
There are people who are

00:08:22.635 --> 00:08:24.237
wrongfully detained.

00:08:24.237 --> 00:08:24.971
The James Foley

00:08:24.971 --> 00:08:25.838
Foundation says

00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:28.107
that there are at least 60 Americans

00:08:28.307 --> 00:08:29.375
wrongfully detained.

00:08:29.375 --> 00:08:31.310
One of them is Austin Tice,

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:33.579
who is a former journalist

00:08:33.579 --> 00:08:34.480
and a former Marine.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:35.781
He was kidnaped in Syria

00:08:35.781 --> 00:08:37.216
more than a year ago.

00:08:37.216 --> 00:08:39.785
I know you personally traveled to Syria.

00:08:39.785 --> 00:08:42.822
Any update on his situation?

00:08:42.822 --> 00:08:45.458
Is he possibly going to come home soon?

00:08:45.458 --> 00:08:46.759
We're still working on that.

00:08:46.759 --> 00:08:48.361
Again, I hate to get into negotiations

00:08:48.361 --> 00:08:49.929
and things that we're currently doing.

00:08:49.929 --> 00:08:51.197
But as I've often told

00:08:51.197 --> 00:08:53.699
Deborah Tice's mother, I'm optimistic.

00:08:53.699 --> 00:08:55.201
I think there are always pass forward

00:08:55.201 --> 00:08:56.736
that allow us to get an opportunity

00:08:56.736 --> 00:08:58.938
to bring someone like Austin home.

00:08:58.938 --> 00:09:00.873
Well, you have helped bring home

00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:02.341
15 Americans

00:09:02.341 --> 00:09:03.543
who were wrongfully detained

00:09:03.543 --> 00:09:05.778
or held hostage in nine months.

00:09:05.778 --> 00:09:06.979
That's a pretty good track record.

00:09:06.979 --> 00:09:09.148
And we thank you so much for your service

00:09:09.148 --> 00:09:10.650
and for coming out and telling the story.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:12.218
Thank you for having me. On. Thank you.

